Hi Colton,

Can you clarify what rjProb() does and so what z[1:maxlag] values will be? Are they indicators of before vs after "stable"?

In general, what you are saying makes sense. When the change point is proposed to a new value, a joint proposal with one of more of the "lag" values could consider values jointly with the change point proposal.

At a general glance your sampler makes sense, but I have not taken the time to try to vouch that it is correct. One thing I could see needing care is whether there are a different number of lag proposals when proposing a later vs earlier value of stable, and needing to ensure the reverse and forward probabilities are accounted carefully.

Off the top of my head I don't have a great sampling suggestion. If maxlag is not huge, you could marginalize over lag by noting that lag[1:maxlag] and all response[] values are jointly multivariate normal. Another way to look at the same thing would be to use a Kalman Filter to calculate the marginal likelihood of the response[] values (even for large maxlag values).

HTH
Perry

